        <div id="test-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Do You Test Your Water Before Buying a Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with numbers, not products: a utility report or a certified lab panel tells you what the system has to remove. On city water, download your utility's annual <strong>Consumer Confidence Report</strong>, which lists every regulated contaminant found at the treatment plant and the level it was found at. It covers chlorine or chloramine, disinfection byproducts, PFAS, nitrate and hardness at the source. It cannot see what happens inside your own plumbing, so add a lead test at the kitchen tap if the house was built before 1986.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Private wells: test every year</h3>
          <p>
            On a private well, testing is your job. The EPA and most state health departments advise testing at least once a year for total coliform bacteria, nitrate, pH and total dissolved solids, and every few years for arsenic, uranium, iron, manganese and hardness, depending on where you live. Use a state-certified laboratory instead of a hardware-store strip kit. A lab panel typically costs $50 to $300 and gives you figures you can size a system against.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What strip kits and TDS pens can and cannot tell you</h3>
          <p>
            Strip kits and TDS pens are good for watching trends, not for choosing equipment. A TDS reading of 350 ppm says a lot is dissolved in the water, but not whether it is harmless calcium or something worth worrying about. A hardness strip can confirm you need a softener, yet it says nothing about lead or PFAS, which are measured in parts per billion or parts per trillion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Turn the results into a shopping list</h3>
          <p>
            Write the results down and mark anything at or close to a federal limit or a nuisance threshold. Iron above about 0.3 milligrams per liter leaves orange stains on fixtures. Hardness above roughly 7 grains per gallon builds scale. Hydrogen sulfide is behind the sulfur smell. Each of those points to a particular kind of equipment, and the marked-up list becomes your shopping list for the rest of this guide.
          </p>

