        <div id="poe-vs-pou" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Do You Need a Whole-House Filter or a Point-of-Use Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It depends on where the problem shows up: treat at the main line for anything that affects showers, appliances or fixtures, and at the sink for anything that only matters when you drink it. A <strong>point-of-entry</strong> system, usually called whole-house, is installed on the main line after the meter or pressure tank and treats all the water entering the home, from showers and the washing machine to the water heater and every faucet. A <strong>point-of-use</strong> system treats a single tap, usually the kitchen sink, for drinking and cooking only. Most homes need one or the other; a few need both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When whole-house treatment makes sense</h3>
          <p>
            Choose whole-house when the problem reaches beyond the glass. Chlorine or chloramine that dries out skin and hair, sediment that clogs aerators and appliance valves, iron that stains toilets and laundry, and a sulfur smell in the shower are all point-of-entry problems. Hard water scale belongs on that list too, although it calls for a softener rather than a filter. A large carbon tank also takes care of chlorine taste at every tap, so nobody needs a pitcher in every room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a sink filter is enough</h3>
          <p>
            Choose point-of-use when the worry is something you swallow. Lead, PFAS, arsenic, nitrate and fluoride matter when ingested, not in the shower. Treating them throughout the house is expensive and wasteful, because perhaps one or two percent of a home's water is actually consumed. An under-sink reverse osmosis unit reduces a broad range of dissolved contaminants for a fraction of what a whole-house system with the same reach would cost.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The combination most homes end up with</h3>
          <p>
            The usual pairing is a whole-house sediment and carbon system for comfort and appliance protection, plus an under-sink RO or carbon block filter for drinking water. If you rent, cannot change the plumbing or drink only a few liters a day, a countertop RO unit or a certified pitcher covers the drinking side. Our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">water filter pitcher rankings</a> cover the least expensive end of that range.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">If it bothers you in the shower or marks your fixtures, treat it where the water enters the house. If it only matters when you drink it, treat it at the sink.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Families who overspend usually bought whole-house treatment for a drinking-water contaminant. Families who end up unhappy usually bought a sink filter for a problem that runs through every pipe.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="system-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Types of Home Water Filtration Systems Are There?
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are five main types, sediment filters, carbon systems, reverse osmosis, softeners and specialty well equipment such as iron filters and UV, and each one deals with a different set of problems. Most real installations chain two or more of them together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sediment filters</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Sediment filters</strong> come first in nearly every system. They trap sand, rust flakes and silt and are rated in microns, usually 5 to 50 for whole-house use. Finer is not automatically better: on a sandy well, a 5-micron cartridge can clog within weeks. Many installers put a coarse spin-down screen first and a finer pleated cartridge after it. Sediment filtration shields every stage downstream and the appliances behind them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon systems</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Carbon systems</strong> come in two formats. Cartridge housings, such as the 20-inch big blue housings, are inexpensive and easy to service but give the water limited contact time. Tank systems hold a cubic foot or more of granular or catalytic carbon and run for years between media changes. Catalytic carbon is the one to pick for chloramine, which standard carbon handles poorly. Carbon takes care of taste, odor, chlorine and many organic chemicals, but not hardness, nitrate or dissolved minerals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reverse osmosis</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Reverse osmosis</strong> uses household water pressure to push water through a very tight membrane, removing most dissolved solids, including lead, arsenic, fluoride, nitrate, many PFAS compounds and salts. Units certified to NSF/ANSI 58 have independently verified performance. Because RO is slow and sends part of the water to drain, it goes at the sink rather than on the whole house. Tankless models with built-in pumps shorten the wait and improve the waste ratio.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Softeners, iron filters and UV</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Softeners</strong> rely on ion exchange resin to trade calcium and magnesium for sodium or potassium. They stop scale but filter nothing else. <strong>Iron filters</strong> oxidize dissolved iron, manganese and hydrogen sulfide, often using an air pocket in the tank, then backwash the particles out. <strong>UV systems</strong> disinfect by scrambling microbial DNA, but they only work on clear water free of sediment, so they always sit at the end of the chain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Size Whole-House Water Filter Do I Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Size it for peak demand, meaning the flow when the most fixtures could run at once, because an undersized system is the most common cause of low-pressure complaints. A shower uses about 2 gallons per minute, a washing machine 2 to 3, a dishwasher 1 to 2 and a kitchen faucet about 1.5. A two-bathroom house with the laundry going can easily reach 8 to 10 gpm. Pick a system rated at or above that peak, not the average.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Match the port size to the main line</h3>
          <p>
            Port size counts as well. Most homes have three-quarter-inch or one-inch main lines, and a system with three-quarter-inch ports on a one-inch line becomes a bottleneck. Larger homes with three or more bathrooms usually need one-inch ports and a rating of 12 gpm or more. Makers typically offer the same system in several sizes by bathroom count, so choose by bathroom count and then check the flow rating against your own calculation.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity: how long the media lasts</h3>
          <p>
            Capacity is the other half of sizing. Whole-house carbon tanks are rated in gallons or years, commonly 500,000 to 1,000,000 gallons for a full tank; at 100 gallons per person per day, that is several years for a family of four. Cartridge systems carry much lower ratings, commonly 50,000 to 100,000 gallons for the carbon stage. Sediment cartridges have no fixed rating and last until the pressure drop says they are full.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sizing an under-sink RO system</h3>
          <p>
            For under-sink RO, compare the gallons-per-day rating and whether the unit uses a tank or a pump. Tank units rated for 50 to 75 gallons per day slowly fill a storage tank and dispense from it, so a few gallons come quickly and then you wait. Tankless units rated for 400 to 800 gallons per day make water on demand and fill a glass in seconds. Waste ratio, the water sent to drain per gallon produced, runs from about 3 to 1 on older tank units to near 1 to 1 or better on modern tankless designs.
          </p>

