        <div id="well-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Is the Best Water Filtration System for Well Water?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best well setup is a chain built in a fixed order from your lab results rather than a single product: sediment filtration first, then iron and manganese removal, a softener if hardness is high, carbon for taste, and UV disinfection last. The order matters because each stage protects the next. Iron fouls softener resin, hardness scales the quartz sleeve in a UV unit, and any cloudiness gives microbes a place to hide from UV light. Get the order wrong and you ruin equipment.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How much iron is too much?</h3>
          <p>
            Iron defines the system on many wells. Below about 1 ppm, a softener can cope with small amounts. From roughly 1 to 7 ppm, an air-injection oxidizing filter is the standard answer, and it also handles moderate hydrogen sulfide. Above that range, or where iron bacteria are present, chemical injection with chlorine or hydrogen peroxide plus a contact tank is usually needed. These systems backwash, so plan for a drain and an electrical outlet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to do if bacteria show up</h3>
          <p>
            Coliform calls for a specific sequence. First, shock chlorinate the well and track down the source, such as a damaged well cap or poor grouting. If the contamination keeps coming back, a UV system sized to peak flow gives continuous disinfection. For systems that claim to disinfect contaminated water, look for NSF/ANSI 55 Class A. Replace UV lamps about once a year, whether or not they still glow.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Arsenic, uranium and nitrate</h3>
          <p>
            Arsenic, uranium and nitrate are drinking-water problems, and on a well an under-sink RO system certified for those contaminants is usually more practical than treating the whole house. Retest after installing anything, because a system you cannot prove is working is just a tank in the basement. If the space where the equipment lives is also damp, our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> helps protect the electronics in control valves and UV ballasts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do NSF/ANSI Certifications Mean for a Home Water Filter?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A certification means an accredited body such as NSF, IAPMO, WQA or CSA has confirmed the maker's claim and audits the factory, which makes it the most useful line on any filter's spec sheet. Filtration marketing is crowded with percentages that lack context, and a listing cuts through them. Each standard applies to a particular kind of device and a particular list of contaminants, so a certification helps only if it names the substance you care about. Look the product up in the certifier's online database rather than trusting the box.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The standards to know</h3>
          <p>
            For carbon filters, <strong>NSF/ANSI 42</strong> covers chlorine, taste, odor and particulates, while <strong>NSF/ANSI 53</strong> covers health contaminants such as lead, cysts, VOCs and, in newer listings, PFOA and PFOS. <strong>NSF/ANSI 401</strong> adds incidental compounds like certain pharmaceuticals and herbicides. <strong>NSF/ANSI 58</strong> is the reverse osmosis standard, covering TDS reduction plus optional claims for arsenic, nitrate, fluoride and others. <strong>NSF/ANSI 55</strong> applies to UV disinfection, and <strong>NSF/ANSI 372</strong> confirms low-lead materials.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why many whole-house systems lack a full listing</h3>
          <p>
            Whole-house systems are the weak spot. Many large tank systems are run through NSF protocols by a third-party lab without holding a full listing, because certifying point-of-entry equipment is slow and costly. That alone is not a red flag, but it means you should ask for the lab report itself and check which contaminants and challenge levels it used. A report that covers only chlorine at a modest level says little about anything else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insist on a real listing at the sink</h3>
          <p>
            For drinking-water systems at the sink, accept nothing less than a real listing. Under-sink RO and carbon block filters are widely certified, so there is little reason to settle for one that is not. If your test shows lead or PFAS, the data sheet should show reduction of that contaminant at the standard's challenge level, along with rated capacity in gallons. Replace the filter at that capacity, because the certification covers performance only up to it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Can You Install a Whole-House Water Filter Yourself?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, if you are comfortable cutting into the main line and fitting a bypass loop; otherwise a plumber typically charges $300 to $1,000 for a standard system. Under-sink systems are easier and a fair DIY job for anyone with basic plumbing skills. A carbon block filter usually tees into the cold-water supply and needs its own faucet, which can mean drilling the sink or countertop. RO units add a drain saddle on the sink drain line, and some need an outlet nearby for a booster pump. Allow a couple of hours and read the manual first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What a whole-house install involves</h3>
          <p>
            A whole-house install is a bigger commitment. You cut into the main line, fit a bypass loop with three valves so the system can be serviced without shutting off the house, and add shutoffs on each side. Copper calls for soldering or push-fit fittings, and PEX needs the correct crimp or expansion tools. Many local codes require a permit for work on the main supply line. If those steps are unfamiliar, hire the plumber.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do not skip the bonding jumper</h3>
          <p>
            Grounding is the detail people overlook. Many homes use metal water pipes as part of the electrical grounding path, and a plastic filter housing or plastic bypass valve can break that path. A code-compliant install adds a <strong>bonding jumper</strong> across the system, clamped on each side. It is an inexpensive wire and clamp, and leaving it out can leave the metal plumbing unbonded. If you have other electrical work planned, our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole house surge protector guide</a> covers the main panel side of the same project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pick the location before you buy</h3>
          <p>
            Choose the spot before ordering. Whole-house tanks are tall and heavy once full, and backwashing models need a drain within reach and an outlet. Keep systems indoors, protected from freezing and direct sun, ideally after the pressure tank on a well and ahead of the water heater. Leave room to change cartridges and lift off the tank head, because a system crammed into a closet makes every future service visit miserable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How Much Does a Home Water Filtration System Cost?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expect anywhere from about $200 for a basic under-sink RO unit to $4,000 for a well combination system before installation, at the time of writing, and the upkeep can matter as much as the sticker. A three-stage cartridge whole-house system runs roughly $250 to $600, and a tank-style whole-house carbon system roughly $1,000 to $2,500. Well water combinations with iron filtration and UV land around $1,500 to $4,000 before installation. Under-sink RO runs from about $200 for a basic tank unit to $600 or more for tankless models, and countertop RO roughly $300 to $500.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Running costs change the ranking</h3>
          <p>
            Ongoing costs can flip the order. Cartridge whole-house systems need fresh sediment and carbon cartridges every three to twelve months, often $100 to $250 a year. Tank systems need only a sediment pre-filter for years, then a media change costing a few hundred dollars every five to ten years. Under-sink RO pre-filters cost roughly $40 to $100 a year, with a new membrane every two to four years, and UV lamps run roughly $60 to $150 a year.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do the ten-year math</h3>
          <p>
            Work out a ten-year figure before deciding. A $400 cartridge system with $200 a year in filters comes to $2,400 over a decade. A $1,600 tank system with a $150 sediment budget and one media change can end up costing about the same, and it asks less of you. If you tend to forget filter changes, the system with the fewest service intervals usually delivers better water in real life.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">System</th>
