        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. How Often Should You Change Home Water Filters?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most homes change sediment cartridges every three to six months and carbon cartridges every six to twelve, but pressure gauges tell you more than the calendar does. Fit a gauge before and after a whole-house system; they cost a few dollars, and when the pressure drop across the system grows by about 10 to 15 psi past its clean baseline, the sediment cartridge is loading up. Without gauges, the first sign is usually a weak shower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A typical schedule</h3>
          <p>
            Set calendar reminders from the manual. A common schedule looks like this: sediment cartridges every three to six months, carbon cartridges every six to twelve months, RO pre-filters every six to twelve months, the RO membrane every two to four years, and the UV lamp every twelve months with the quartz sleeve cleaned on the same visit. Tank media lasts for years, but check the control valve settings once a year and after any power outage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sanitize at every change</h3>
          <p>
            Sanitize each time you swap filters. Shut off the supply, release the pressure, clean the housings with a mild unscented bleach solution or the maker's sanitizer, rinse well and lubricate the O-rings with food-grade silicone grease. A dry or twisted O-ring is the main reason for slow leaks after a cartridge change, and the original eventually flattens, so keep a spare on hand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Retest once a year</h3>
          <p>
            Retest every year, because filter media gives no warning when it fails, and a fresh lab test or tap lead test confirms the system still does its job. On a well, also test after any flood or long power outage. Keep a log beside the system with install dates, filter changes and test results; it helps a plumber diagnose problems quickly and helps a buyer if you sell the house.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes With Home Water Filtration
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying before testing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every system targets specific contaminants. Skip the utility report or lab test and you end up choosing by marketing, which is how people buy carbon when they needed a softener, or a softener when they needed lead removal. A lab panel costs less than one set of replacement filters and makes the decision for you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Undersizing a whole-house system</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Put a system rated for 7 gpm in a house that peaks at 12 gpm and pressure sags whenever two showers and the washer run together. Count the fixtures, total their flow at peak, and match the port size to the main line. Choosing by bathroom count and then checking the flow math prevents it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the bypass loop</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                With no bypass, every cartridge swap or valve repair cuts water to the entire house. Three valves and a handful of fittings let the house keep running while you service the system, and they let you isolate it quickly if it starts to leak. Plumbers fit one by default, and that habit exists for good reason.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the bonding jumper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many houses use metal water pipe as part of the electrical grounding path. A plastic filter housing interrupts it unless a bonding jumper is clamped across the system. The fix is a short length of copper wire and two clamps, and it keeps the metal plumbing bonded the way code requires. Check it before closing up the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting UV before sediment and iron filtration</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                UV can only disinfect water it can shine through. Sediment, iron and hardness shade microbes from the light and coat the quartz sleeve. Always install UV as the final stage, after the sediment, iron and softening equipment, and clean the sleeve each year when you replace the lamp.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

