        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which home water filtration setup suits most households?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On city water, a whole-house catalytic carbon tank such as the SpringWell CF1, combined with an under-sink reverse osmosis unit for drinking water, covers most concerns. When money is tight, a three-stage cartridge system like the iSpring WGB32B deals with sediment and chlorine. Let your utility report or lab test make the final call, not the marketing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a whole-house filter remove lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably. Most whole-house carbon systems are not certified for lead, and lead often leaches from solder, faucets and fittings downstream of the filter anyway. The dependable answer is an NSF/ANSI 53 or 58 certified filter at the kitchen tap, plus replacing lead service lines where you can. A few whole-house systems offer lead-specific stages, so check their certifications first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a whole-house system lower my water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A properly sized system with clean filters costs you only a small pressure drop. Trouble comes from undersized systems, ports narrower than the main line, or clogged sediment cartridges. Match the rated flow to your peak household demand, fit pressure gauges on both sides, and change the sediment filter once the pressure difference grows noticeably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a softener and a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If your water is both hard and chlorinated, yes, because they do different jobs. A softener removes the calcium and magnesium behind scale, while a carbon filter removes chlorine, off tastes and many chemicals. On chlorine or chloramine water, put the carbon filter ahead of the softener, since chlorine wears down softener resin over time.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does professional installation cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the time of writing, plumbers typically charge roughly $150 to $400 for an under-sink system and $300 to $1,000 for a standard whole-house filter. Well systems with backwashing tanks, iron filters and UV can cost more, especially when new drains or outlets are needed. Ask for quotes that include a bypass loop and a bonding jumper.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is reverse osmosis water safe to drink long term?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. RO strips minerals along with contaminants, but most of the calcium and magnesium in a diet comes from food, not water. Some people prefer the taste of water with minerals in it, and many RO systems offer a remineralization stage. Keep the filters current, because a spent pre-filter can let chlorine through to damage the membrane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I filter well water with the same system as city water?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. City water mostly needs chlorine or chloramine removed, while well water often needs sediment, iron, manganese and hardness treatment, and sometimes UV disinfection. Get a full lab panel first, then build the chain in order: sediment, iron removal, softener, carbon, and UV last. A city-water carbon tank on its own usually fails quickly on a well.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

